{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019JgYMM3nRxESVdEo8mDeuC","type":"tool_result","content":"170\t          text: -R must list directories in the same sort order as entries within a directory. If -t is active, subdirectories are recursed into in modification-time order.\n171\t\n172\t  R4:\n173\t    title: Exit codes, signal handling, and flag interactions\n174\t    items:\n175\t      - R4.1:\n176\t          text: Must exit 0 when all listed paths are accessed and output is written successfully.\n177\t      - R4.2:\n178\t          text: Must exit 1 when a minor problem occurs, such as failing to access a file or directory given as an argument (e.g., permission denied, file not found). Must still list the remaining accessible entries before exiting. Must print a diagnostic to stderr for each inaccessible entry.\n179\t      - R4.3:\n180\t          text: Must exit 2 when a serious problem occurs, such as an invalid command-line option. This matches GNU ls (ls.c:5638 exit(status) where status is set to EXIT_FAILURE=2 for bad options at ls.c:2026).\n181\t      - R4.4:\n182\t          text: |\n183\t            Must install a SIGPIPE handler at startup so that piping ls output to head or other truncating consumers does not produce a broken-pipe error message. Implementation: call pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() — this uses signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of size 1; a dedicated goroutine calls os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Must not use signal.Ignore (deferred cleanup functions must not run on SIGPIPE, matching GNU ls behavior).\n184\t      - R4.5:\n185\t          text: Must install a SIGWINCH handler via pkg/sys.OnTerminalResize(callback func(width int)) that re-queries the terminal width and updates the column layout. pkg/sys.OnTerminalResize registers a SIGWINCH listener that calls pkg/sys.TerminalWidth() and invokes the callback with the new width; multiple callbacks may be registered. This allows ls to adapt column layout if the terminal is resized during a long listing.\n186\t      - R4.6:\n187\t          text: -n implies -l. When -n is given without -l, the output must be in long format with numeric UID/GID.\n188\t      - R4.7:\n189\t          text: -C and -x are mutually exclusive with -l and -1. The last format flag on the command line wins (see R1.14).\n190\t      - R4.8:\n191\t          text: -R with -l must produce a \"total N\" block line for each subdirectory listing, matching GNU ls -lR output.\n192\t      - R4.9:\n193\t          text: -i, -s, and -F may all be combined with -R. Each subdirectory listing applies the same metadata display and classification options.\n194\t\n195\tnon_goals:\n196\t  - --hyperlink (terminal hyperlinks) is out of scope for this project.\n197\t  - --dired (Emacs dired mode) is out of scope.\n198\t  - -Z (SELinux security context) is out of scope; this project does not target SELinux.\n199\t  - --quoting-style (filename quoting) is out of scope for this release."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"790c8c18-fa47-4616-8f38-b776535ef573","uuid":"b06a5034-99d9-4b53-a9cf-f555c19bf015","timestamp":"2026-05-14T22:43:48.814Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5223/docs/specs/software-requirements/srd008-ls.yaml","content":"          text: -R must list directories in the same sort order as entries within a directory. If -t is active, subdirectories are recursed into in modification-time order.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Exit codes, signal handling, and flag interactions\n    items:\n      - R4.1:\n          text: Must exit 0 when all listed paths are accessed and output is written successfully.\n      - R4.2:\n          text: Must exit 1 when a minor problem occurs, such as failing to access a file or directory given as an argument (e.g., permission denied, file not found). Must still list the remaining accessible entries before exiting. Must print a diagnostic to stderr for each inaccessible entry.\n      - R4.3:\n          text: Must exit 2 when a serious problem occurs, such as an invalid command-line option. This matches GNU ls (ls.c:5638 exit(status) where status is set to EXIT_FAILURE=2 for bad options at ls.c:2026).\n      - R4.4:\n          text: |\n            Must install a SIGPIPE handler at startup so that piping ls output to head or other truncating consumers does not produce a broken-pipe error message. Implementation: call pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() — this uses signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of size 1; a dedicated goroutine calls os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Must not use signal.Ignore (deferred cleanup functions must not run on SIGPIPE, matching GNU ls behavior).\n      - R4.5:\n          text: Must install a SIGWINCH handler via pkg/sys.OnTerminalResize(callback func(width int)) that re-queries the terminal width and updates the column layout. pkg/sys.OnTerminalResize registers a SIGWINCH listener that calls pkg/sys.TerminalWidth() and invokes the callback with the new width; multiple callbacks may be registered. This allows ls to adapt column layout if the terminal is resized during a long listing.\n      - R4.6:\n          text: -n implies -l. When -n is given without -l, the output must be in long format with numeric UID/GID.\n      - R4.7:\n          text: -C and -x are mutually exclusive with -l and -1. The last format flag on the command line wins (see R1.14).\n      - R4.8:\n          text: -R with -l must produce a \"total N\" block line for each subdirectory listing, matching GNU ls -lR output.\n      - R4.9:\n          text: -i, -s, and -F may all be combined with -R. Each subdirectory listing applies the same metadata display and classification options.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - --hyperlink (terminal hyperlinks) is out of scope for this project.\n  - --dired (Emacs dired mode) is out of scope.\n  - -Z (SELinux security context) is out