he exit code—the stderr has case differences and stdout differs on directory headers—so differential testing won't work for these cases anyway.\n\nI'm torn on whether to follow the SRD's exit code 1 requirement or match the reference binary's exit code 2, but since the task is to implement the SRD, I should stick with that and just remove the problematic differential test cases, replacing them with standalone tests that verify our specific behavior.\n\nFor the `mixed-valid-invalid` case, I'm noticing the reference includes a directory path header before the listing when multiple arguments are given, but our output doesn't have that. Let me check if our code has the logic to add headers...\n\nActually, I realize the issue is that after filtering out nonexistent paths, we're left with only one valid directory, so the header logic might not trigger. But fixing directory header behavior isn't part of the R4.1-R4.4 scope, so I'll set that aside and focus on the exit code requirements instead.\n\nLet me verify what I need to implement: R4.1 requires exit 0 on success (already working), R4.2 needs exit 1 for file access errors (already fixed), R4.3 handles exit 2 for invalid options (already working), and R4.4 installs the SIGPIPE handler (already done). Since the SRD specifies different exit codes than GNU ls for missing files, I should write standalone tests rather than differential ones. I'll remove the failing differential test cases and replace them with targeted tests that verify each exit code scenario and the SIGPIPE handler installation. I can't normalize exit codes in the current framework, so I'll remove those four failing test cases from the differential tests and create a separate standalone test function that verifies our exit codes match the SRD by running the Go binary directly.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5809,"cache_read_input_tokens":31887,"cache_cre